00:02:48.570 --> 00:02:56.320
On the evening of July 4th,

00:02:56.320 --> 00:02:58.170
1854, an immense

00:02:58.170 --> 00:03:00.000
crowd gathered on the Boston Common

00:03:00.120 --> 00:03:03.880
to celebrate the 78th anniversary of American independence.

00:03:06.160 --> 00:03:10.340
There was almost no one left to remember the revolution.

00:03:10.340 --> 00:03:12.720
As those years of turmoil and hardship

00:03:12.720 --> 00:03:15.290
passed from living memory into myth,

00:03:15.290 --> 00:03:20.860
the union itself came to seem ever more precious.

00:03:20.860 --> 00:03:24.050
Meanwhile, a few miles outside the city,

00:03:24.050 --> 00:03:26.930
anti-slavery activists marked the occasion

00:03:26.930 --> 00:03:28.570
in a very different spirit.

00:03:30.480 --> 00:03:36.470
The government is in the hands of the party demagogues who are the tools of the slave power.

00:03:36.470 --> 00:03:42.550
Corruption is general, constitutional restraints are as cobwebs, the representatives are the

00:03:42.550 --> 00:03:45.150
betrayers of the people.

00:03:45.150 --> 00:03:50.580
William Lloyd Garrison had been waging war against slavery for 25 years.

00:03:50.580 --> 00:03:55.130
But tonight, victory seemed inconceivable.

00:03:55.130 --> 00:04:00.000
Just a few weeks earlier, the President of the United States had sent a massive detachment

00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:04.710
federal troops to Boston, all to return a lone runaway slave to captivity.

00:04:06.370 --> 00:04:11.590
For Garrison, it was yet more proof that the republic itself had been corrupted at birth,

00:04:12.420 --> 00:04:17.160
its original sin implanted in a founding document written largely by slaveholders.

00:04:17.160 --> 00:04:25.460
And this, the Constitution of the United States of America, is the source and parent

00:04:25.460 --> 00:04:30.000
of all the other atrocities, a covenant with death.

00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:41.480
death and an agreement with hell. So perish all compromises with tyranny. And let all

00:04:41.480 --> 00:05:00.000
the people say, Amen. People say, Amen. People say, Amen. Garrison had launched his

00:05:00.000 --> 00:05:10.200
crusade in 1829. Since then, the slave population of the United States had doubled to 4 million.

00:05:10.200 --> 00:05:15.430
The Compromise of 1850 had strengthened the grip of slaveholders over the federal government

00:05:15.430 --> 00:05:23.290
and introduced the notorious Fugitive Slave Law. And now, a new battleground was opening

00:05:23.290 --> 00:05:30.000
up. On America's western frontier, northerners and southerners had come

00:05:30.000 --> 00:05:34.740
come to believe that the nation's destiny would be determined there, as new territories

00:05:34.740 --> 00:05:40.940
were admitted to the Union as either slave or free states.

00:05:40.940 --> 00:05:45.420
In 1855, the flashpoint was Kansas.

00:05:45.420 --> 00:05:48.960
Partisans on each side of the struggle perceived a mortal threat.

00:05:48.960 --> 00:05:55.300
Whichever way Kansas went, the territories further west would likely go the same way.

00:05:55.300 --> 00:06:00.000
Congress had decided to let the settlers in Kansas decide the question for themselves.

00:06:01.760 --> 00:06:03.560
The result was mayhem.

00:06:03.560 --> 00:06:12.670
In the summer of 1855, Kansas has really become the front line in the conflict over slavery.

00:06:12.670 --> 00:06:16.400
Pro-slavery forces really have the upper hand.

00:06:16.400 --> 00:06:21.380
They're bullying and intimidating and sometimes killing northern settlers.

00:06:21.380 --> 00:06:26.760
Many abolitionists wondered if their pacifist ideals were misguided,

00:06:26.760 --> 00:06:29.890
whether the conflict had entered a new and violent phase.

00:06:31.470 --> 00:06:38.940
One of them had no doubt.

00:06:38.940 --> 00:06:56.680
John Brown had never put much stock in Garrison's talk of peace and persuasion.

00:06:56.680 --> 00:07:00.000
"Milk and water abolitionism," he called it.

00:07:02.110 --> 00:07:09.250
And now, at last, John Brown was on his way to war in Kansas.

00:07:09.250 --> 00:07:15.830
Over the years, Brown had attracted a network of abolitionist supporters.

00:07:15.830 --> 00:07:21.900
As he headed west from his home in upstate New York, he collected money and weapons for the fight.

00:07:21.900 --> 00:07:29.410
Among those who contributed was the most famous black man in America, the ex-slave Frederick Douglass.

00:07:30.830 --> 00:07:36.070
"While I will continue to write and speak against slavery," Douglas wrote,

00:07:36.070 --> 00:07:40.180
"I have become less hopeful of its peaceful abolition.

00:07:40.180 --> 00:07:46.620
I welcome any new mode of attack upon the slave system."

00:07:46.620 --> 00:07:51.510
Brown is almost a battering ram of a human being.

00:07:51.510 --> 00:07:57.830
He has a moral strength and clarity that cuts through the cant about slavery.

00:07:57.830 --> 00:08:00.000
And I think this is very appealing to anti-slavery.

00:08:00.000 --> 00:08:06.480
slavery northerners who are really giving up almost on the political system and on non-violent

00:08:06.480 --> 00:08:07.600
resistance.

00:08:07.600 --> 00:08:14.390
By the time Brown left Ohio for the last leg of the journey to eastern Kansas, he had gathered

00:08:14.390 --> 00:08:20.990
a formidable arsenal of rifles, pistols, and broadswords.

00:08:20.990 --> 00:08:26.870
We believe the great victory will follow before long, he wrote home to his wife.

00:08:26.870 --> 00:08:30.000
But John Brown's long and bloody struggle was

00:08:30.000 --> 00:08:48.350
beginning. On the 21st of May 1856, a posse of 800 Southerners surrounded the

00:08:48.350 --> 00:08:54.330
free soil capital of Lawrence, Kansas. They flew a blood-red flag on which was

00:08:54.330 --> 00:09:00.000
inscribed "Southern Rights." Over the course of the day, the

00:09:00.000 --> 00:09:06.450
They sack the town while the inhabitants fled.

00:09:06.450 --> 00:09:11.980
News of the guerrilla war flaring in Kansas became the talk of the nation.

00:09:11.980 --> 00:09:19.750
Northerners began sending clothing and food to the embattled free soil settlers.

00:09:19.750 --> 00:09:25.860
In Washington, anti-slavery Senator Charles Sumner decried the spread of slavery to Kansas

00:09:25.860 --> 00:09:30.000
and accused southerners of "raping and plundering the virgin territory."

00:09:33.000 --> 00:09:37.780
South Carolina Congressman Preston Brooks took offense.

00:09:37.780 --> 00:09:40.370
On the floor of the United States Senate,

00:09:40.370 --> 00:09:44.280
Brooks beat Sumner with the gold head of his heavy cane

00:09:44.280 --> 00:09:48.370
as Sumner struggled to free himself from his desk.

00:09:48.370 --> 00:09:51.680
Finally, blinded by his own blood,

00:09:51.680 --> 00:09:55.190
Sumner collapsed into the aisle and lost consciousness.

00:09:55.190 --> 00:09:58.360
Brooks continued beating him until his cane broke.

00:10:00.000 --> 00:10:05.540
Sumner would never fully recover.

00:10:05.540 --> 00:10:10.580
Newspapers across the south celebrated the assault, but when news of the incident reached

00:10:10.580 --> 00:10:16.310
John Brown's encampment in Kansas, he and his followers were enraged.

00:10:16.310 --> 00:10:22.030
When someone urged caution, Brown replied, "Caution, caution.

00:10:22.030 --> 00:10:24.580
It is nothing but the word of cowardice."

00:10:30.570 --> 00:10:38.820
On the night of May 24, 1856, Brown and four of his sons dragged five pro-slavery men from

00:10:38.820 --> 00:10:45.280
their cabins and hacked them to pieces with broadswords.

00:10:45.280 --> 00:10:50.220
Brown is not simply evening the score in Kansas.

00:10:50.220 --> 00:10:56.730
He's really also trying to strike terror into the hearts of pro-slavery settlers.

00:10:56.730 --> 00:10:58.470
This is God's punishment.

00:10:58.470 --> 00:11:00.000
This is not the punishment of man.

00:11:00.000 --> 00:11:04.050
they're not going to be hung. They're going to be murdered. They're going to be butchered because

00:11:04.050 --> 00:11:12.940
that's Old Testament retribution. Potawatomi scares but also enrages pro-slavery forces who

00:11:12.940 --> 00:11:18.780
don't need much incitement to violence. They've already been committing it and now it's let loose

00:11:18.780 --> 00:11:24.930
the dogs of war. It's at this point that Kansas becomes known as Bleeding Kansas.

00:11:26.860 --> 00:11:30.000
After the massacre, Brown and his followers hid

00:11:30.000 --> 00:11:36.200
out in the wilderness, resurfacing occasionally to battle pro-slavery forces.

00:11:36.200 --> 00:11:46.770
At the beginning of October 1856 he left Kansas for the East to gather more money and weapons.

00:11:46.770 --> 00:11:52.030
It was on a covert swing through Boston that John Brown finally met William Lloyd Garrison

00:11:52.030 --> 00:11:54.200
for the first time.

00:11:54.200 --> 00:12:00.000
The pacifist printer chastised Brown for his role in the Potawatomi killings and insisted

00:12:00.000 --> 00:12:07.570
that non-violence was still the only path to victory. Garrison would have been even more wary

00:12:07.570 --> 00:12:14.000
had he known the full extent of Brown's plans. I will carry the war into Africa,

00:12:14.680 --> 00:12:30.000
Brown had told his son. Africa was their code word for the slave-holding South. On the

00:12:30.000 --> 00:12:37.240
On the morning of March 7, 1857, the papers brought stunning news: out of the blue, the

00:12:37.240 --> 00:12:42.590
Supreme Court had radically altered not just the battle over slavery, but the status of

00:12:42.590 --> 00:12:47.190
every black person in America.

00:12:47.190 --> 00:12:49.150
The case had seemed inconsequential.

00:12:49.150 --> 00:12:55.340
A Missouri slave named Dred Scott wanted the court to set him free, because his master

00:12:55.340 --> 00:13:00.000
had taken him to live in Illinois and then Wisconsin Territory, where slaves had had

00:13:00.000 --> 00:13:07.530
was illegal. Chief Justice Roger Taney saw the case as an opportunity to settle

00:13:07.530 --> 00:13:14.110
the question of slavery once and for all. In a sweeping decision, Taney ruled that

00:13:14.110 --> 00:13:18.230
Congress had no authority to prevent the spread of slavery to the territories.

00:13:18.230 --> 00:13:26.610
Most ominously for free blacks like Frederick Douglass, Taney wrote that

00:13:26.610 --> 00:13:30.000
blacks were so far inferior they had no right

00:13:30.190 --> 00:13:35.560
which the white man was bound to respect and that any free black might lawfully be reduced

00:13:35.560 --> 00:13:41.400
to slavery for his benefit.

00:13:41.400 --> 00:13:48.690
The Dred Scott decision had the potential to legalize slavery everywhere in the United

00:13:48.690 --> 00:13:49.700
States.

00:13:49.700 --> 00:13:57.720
In effect, it turns the entire country into a slave nation.

00:13:57.720 --> 00:14:00.000
So many abolitionists after Dred Scott said,

00:14:00.830 --> 00:14:05.630
we've been telling you for decades that there's a slave power conspiracy out there

00:14:05.630 --> 00:14:14.630
of presidents, supreme court members, of congress people, of bankers, of ship owners

00:14:14.630 --> 00:14:23.250
to not only preserve but expand the system of slavery until it dominates every aspect of American society and economy.

00:14:23.250 --> 00:14:28.710
And now here's the supreme court saying slavery has an eternal future in the United States.

00:14:30.160 --> 00:14:36.690
The decision hung like a pall over the abolitionist movement, casting Douglas, most of all, into

00:14:36.690 --> 00:14:37.690
a deep depression.

00:14:37.690 --> 00:14:45.020
With an earnest, aching heart, I have long looked for the realization of the hope of

00:14:45.020 --> 00:14:45.970
my people.

00:14:45.970 --> 00:14:53.490
I have sought in my humble way to see in the distance the white flags of freedom, the time

00:14:53.490 --> 00:15:00.000
at which the cruel bondage of my people should end, but of that time, I could

00:15:02.040 --> 00:15:05.150
- And you can know nothing.

00:15:05.150 --> 00:15:13.640
All is uncertain.

00:15:13.640 --> 00:15:17.280
- It was with a sense of foreboding

00:15:17.280 --> 00:15:19.820
and hopelessness that Frederick Douglass

00:15:19.820 --> 00:15:23.840
responded to an urgent summons in August of 1859

00:15:23.840 --> 00:15:27.890
from his old friend, John Brown.

00:15:27.890 --> 00:15:29.540
Together with Shields Green,

00:15:30.000 --> 00:15:35.660
fugitive he had befriended in rochester douglas quietly made his way to a stone quarry at

00:15:35.660 --> 00:15:43.370
chambersburg pennsylvania mr douglas captain brown i would never have known you sir

00:15:43.370 --> 00:15:52.610
my time has come brown was secretly encamped at a nearby farmhouse with 22 recruits i had

00:15:52.610 --> 00:16:00.000
hoped for more men of course and i believe we have enough to achieve our ends with your help

00:16:01.210 --> 00:16:02.860
What end is that?

00:16:02.860 --> 00:16:09.920
Well, sir, in one stroke, we shall rouse this nation.

00:16:09.920 --> 00:16:15.120
We will deal the slave power such a blow, it shall never recover.

00:16:15.120 --> 00:16:17.460
All with the 22 minutes.

00:16:17.460 --> 00:16:20.540
It takes one spark to light a fire.

00:16:20.540 --> 00:16:24.320
We are the spark that will set this country ablaze.

00:16:24.320 --> 00:16:26.700
But how?

00:16:26.700 --> 00:16:29.440
The armory at Harper's Ferry.

00:16:31.850 --> 00:16:40.830
good lord man yeah you can't be served a hundred thousand rifles what will 22 men do with a hundred

00:16:40.830 --> 00:16:47.460
thousand as i said the spark you know about the spark there are four million men in bondage who

00:16:47.460 --> 00:16:54.320
will fly to our banner not immediately of course but even a few thousand slaves in this vicinity

00:16:54.320 --> 00:17:00.000
will fly to our aid douglas expected brown to unveil a mission to free

00:17:00.000 --> 00:17:06.780
slaves and funnel them north along the mountains to freedom. But when he gets to the stone quarry,

00:17:07.200 --> 00:17:14.330
Brown presents a very different plan. I know. My friend, I have been over this a thousand times.

00:17:15.130 --> 00:17:21.150
I can assure you. And I can assure you that you'll be walking into a perfect steel trap.

00:17:21.450 --> 00:17:28.570
Talking about invading the South and occupying the South and taking over the South, you know,

00:17:28.570 --> 00:17:30.000
sort of building this republic out.

00:17:30.000 --> 00:17:35.660
One mile at a time, and that republic is going to be a new country.

00:17:35.660 --> 00:17:41.400
It will kill you, and it will serve no purpose.

00:17:41.400 --> 00:17:44.530
There'll be a bloodbath.

00:17:44.530 --> 00:17:47.680
Without the shedding of blood, there's

00:17:47.680 --> 00:17:50.940
no remission of sin, Douglas.

00:17:50.940 --> 00:17:54.500
Douglas spends two days trying to convince John Brown

00:17:54.500 --> 00:17:56.840
not to raid Harper's Ferry.

00:17:56.840 --> 00:18:00.000
Brown spends the same amount of time trying

00:18:00.090 --> 00:18:04.650
He convinced Douglas to go to Harper's Ferry with him to be his right-hand man.

00:18:05.190 --> 00:18:07.670
My friend, the world will remember what we do here.

00:18:08.680 --> 00:18:10.380
How do you want the world to remember you?

00:18:12.240 --> 00:18:14.600
How do you want your children to remember you?

00:18:17.180 --> 00:18:20.800
I don't want them to remember me as throwing my life away from nothing.

00:18:22.330 --> 00:18:28.640
Captain, it pains me more than you'll know.

00:18:30.030 --> 00:18:34.010
to leave you.

00:18:34.010 --> 00:18:40.710
Mr. Green, you've heard Mr. Brown.

00:18:40.710 --> 00:18:44.190
What will you do?

00:18:44.190 --> 00:18:50.110
Leave, I'll go with the old man.

00:18:50.110 --> 00:18:51.350
Come with me, Douglas.

00:19:00.640 --> 00:19:02.160
I will defend you with my life.

00:19:06.520 --> 00:19:08.100
I want you for a special purpose.

00:19:10.220 --> 00:19:12.520
When I strike, the bees will begin to swarm.

00:19:13.820 --> 00:19:15.980
And I shall want you to help hive them.

00:19:19.420 --> 00:19:23.840
Frederick Douglass returned home alone.

00:19:26.120 --> 00:19:29.860
The decision to leave Brown would haunt him for the rest of his life.

00:19:46.160 --> 00:19:49.040
On the night of October 16th, 1859,

00:19:50.040 --> 00:19:53.880
John Brown and his men seized the Federal Armory at Harper's Ferry,

00:19:54.490 --> 00:19:55.690
taking several hostages.

00:19:56.940 --> 00:20:00.000
But over the course of the following day, they were overwhelmed.

00:20:00.730 --> 00:20:09.630
as every able-bodied man in the area grabbed a gun and raced into town. Within 24 hours,

00:20:09.630 --> 00:20:17.500
half of Brown's men were dead or dying. He and four of the survivors, including Shields Green,

00:20:17.500 --> 00:20:22.320
were trapped in an engine house at the armory. By this time,

00:20:22.320 --> 00:20:30.000
a company of U.S. Marines had arrived under the command of Colonel Robert E. Lee. Fragmentary reports

00:20:30.000 --> 00:20:36.870
of the uprising spread across the country. In Boston, William Lloyd Garrison wondered

00:20:36.870 --> 00:20:43.860
whether Brown was involved. In Philadelphia, Frederick Douglass knew all too well.

00:20:43.860 --> 00:20:52.500
It so happened that I was speaking to a large audience in National Hall. The announcement

00:20:52.500 --> 00:20:57.580
came upon us with the startling effect of an earthquake. It was something to make the

00:20:57.580 --> 00:20:59.220
boldest hold his breath.

00:21:00.890 --> 00:21:07.090
The sensation Douglass felt at that moment is, "Oh, God, what comes now? Oh, God, they'll

00:21:07.090 --> 00:21:12.550
be after all of us. What sort of bloodbath may flow from this? His own life was at risk

00:21:12.550 --> 00:21:15.130
now."

00:21:15.130 --> 00:21:22.400
At dawn, the Marines stormed the engine house, capturing Brown and his surviving men. Investigators

00:21:22.400 --> 00:21:27.710
seeking a link to the abolitionists found what they needed in Brown's papers. An innocuous

00:21:27.710 --> 00:21:30.000
note signed by Frederick Douglass two years

00:21:30.000 --> 00:21:40.420
earlier inviting brown to dinner it was all they needed douglas suddenly becomes the most wanted

00:21:40.420 --> 00:21:46.660
man in the united states he hides out at a friend's place in philadelphia in fact he finds out from

00:21:46.660 --> 00:21:53.780
the the telegraph operator in philadelphia that the president of the united states on down has

00:21:53.780 --> 00:22:00.000
put out the equivalent of an all points bulletin all the feds are now scouring the

00:22:00.000 --> 00:22:01.680
country for Frederick Douglass.

00:22:01.680 --> 00:22:05.420
Douglass hurriedly made his way north.

00:22:05.420 --> 00:22:09.700
His panic mounting as he realized that his fellow passengers were reading newspaper reports

00:22:09.700 --> 00:22:12.890
calling for his arrest.

00:22:12.890 --> 00:22:17.800
He telegraphed ahead with instructions to destroy the contents of his desk.

00:22:17.800 --> 00:22:24.330
Miraculously, Douglass made it to Rochester, where he boarded a boat to Canada.

00:22:24.330 --> 00:22:30.000
The next day, Douglass heard that federal marshals had arrived at his home, hot on his trail.

00:22:31.050 --> 00:22:35.110
but he was out of their reach.

00:22:35.110 --> 00:22:39.650
Douglass was wanted more than any of the other conspirators

00:22:39.650 --> 00:22:51.600
in John Brown's rave because Douglass was a black man.

00:22:51.600 --> 00:22:54.610
John Brown was almost killed

00:22:54.610 --> 00:22:57.310
when the Marines stormed the engine house.

00:22:57.310 --> 00:23:00.000
He had to be carried from his jail cell to a cot

00:23:00.030 --> 00:23:03.150
that was set up in the courtroom.

00:23:03.150 --> 00:23:09.050
Nevertheless, Brown dominated the proceedings.

00:23:09.050 --> 00:23:11.670
- He stands up without notes

00:23:11.670 --> 00:23:16.120
and gives one of the greatest speeches in American history.

00:23:16.120 --> 00:23:20.290
Brown, in simple, direct, powerful language,

00:23:21.250 --> 00:23:26.710
basically put his accusers on trial.

00:23:26.710 --> 00:23:30.000
- Had I interfered in behalf of the rich, the powerful,

00:23:30.190 --> 00:23:32.720
the intelligent, the so-called "great."

00:23:32.720 --> 00:23:38.260
Every man in this court would have deemed it an act worthy of reward rather than punishment.

00:23:38.260 --> 00:23:48.030
Now, if it is deemed necessary that I should forfeit my life for the furtherance of the ends of justice

00:23:48.030 --> 00:23:54.570
and mingle my blood further with the blood of my children, I submit.

00:23:54.570 --> 00:23:59.890
Shields Green and the other captives had already been sentenced to hang.

00:24:00.350 --> 00:24:03.930
there could be little doubt that Brown would follow them.

00:24:03.930 --> 00:24:08.150
From his jail cell, Brown kept up a stream of correspondence

00:24:08.150 --> 00:24:10.010
and interviews, which were breathlessly

00:24:10.010 --> 00:24:13.640
reported throughout the North.

00:24:13.640 --> 00:24:17.870
This guy's just led a raid to destroy slavery.

00:24:17.870 --> 00:24:20.740
They've killed people, and not the first people he's killed.

00:24:20.740 --> 00:24:23.620
And he's suddenly become famously depicted in art,

00:24:23.620 --> 00:24:26.030
wearing these sort of evening slippers

00:24:26.030 --> 00:24:30.000
with this long, peaceful, old patriarch beard.

00:24:30.000 --> 00:24:33.760
the abolitionist grandfather you wish you had.

00:24:33.760 --> 00:24:39.470
- Brown was absolutely a genius at working the press,

00:24:39.470 --> 00:24:43.150
at giving interviews, talking to people,

00:24:43.150 --> 00:24:48.820
and turning himself into as fully committed

00:24:48.820 --> 00:24:53.970
a Christian sacrificial lamb on the altar of slavery

00:24:53.970 --> 00:24:57.410
as he could possibly be.

00:24:57.410 --> 00:24:59.480
- You have the Northern establishment

00:25:00.000 --> 00:25:03.940
every newspaper saying, "This guy's a hero.

00:25:03.940 --> 00:25:05.380
"He's a saint.

00:25:05.380 --> 00:25:07.380
"How can Southerners look at that and say,

00:25:07.380 --> 00:25:09.380
"Oh, these guys are good negotiating partners.

00:25:09.380 --> 00:25:12.010
"We're going to be able to sort this slavery problem out."

00:25:12.010 --> 00:25:14.010
What Brown has done, ultimately,

00:25:14.010 --> 00:25:18.430
is just to expose the depth of the divide

00:25:18.430 --> 00:25:20.410
between North and South.

00:25:20.410 --> 00:25:26.790
And after that point, that divide can never be bridged.

00:25:30.570 --> 00:25:35.240
- On the morning of December 2nd, 1859,

00:25:35.240 --> 00:25:37.530
John Brown was driven from the jail,

00:25:37.530 --> 00:25:53.900
seated on his own coffin, to the gallows nearby.

00:25:53.900 --> 00:25:56.750
Brown's execution, 4,000 people

00:25:56.750 --> 00:25:59.090
crowded into Boston's Tremont Temple.

00:26:00.700 --> 00:26:10.690
most of them had come to hear william lloyd garrison garrison has to resolve this this tension

00:26:11.130 --> 00:26:22.800
between clearly unapologetic armed resistance and strategy and maneuver with pacifism

00:26:22.800 --> 00:26:30.000
and moral suasion and you can see or we can hear in that speech that he gives he just

00:26:30.120 --> 00:26:33.600
He is truly like a boat in rough sea.

00:26:33.600 --> 00:26:37.950
He's just rocking back and forth.

00:26:37.950 --> 00:26:40.940
- I have labored unremittingly

00:26:40.940 --> 00:26:44.720
to effect the peaceful abolition of slavery

00:26:44.720 --> 00:26:47.600
by an appeal to the reason and conscience

00:26:47.600 --> 00:26:49.380
of the slave holder.

00:26:49.380 --> 00:26:54.290
Yet, as a peaceman, an ultra-peaceman,

00:26:54.290 --> 00:27:00.000
I am prepared to say success to every slave insurrection

00:27:00.000 --> 00:27:11.590
when he started his abolitionist campaign as a printer one could still hope in the early 1830s

00:27:11.590 --> 00:27:20.330
that maybe arranging types in a case stringing words together in descendances publishing articles

00:27:20.330 --> 00:27:28.550
against slavery might persuade the nation to rid itself of slavery but as time goes by garrison

00:27:28.550 --> 00:27:30.000
begins to persuade the nation to rid itself of slavery but as time goes by garrison begins to

00:27:30.000 --> 00:27:35.320
he leapt along by the changing events around him.

00:27:35.320 --> 00:27:39.680
- I think he recognized that this was a turning point.

00:27:39.680 --> 00:27:43.380
Garrison was confronting some things that suggested

00:27:43.380 --> 00:27:45.990
that the future was going to take

00:27:45.990 --> 00:27:47.890
a very different path than the past.

00:28:00.000 --> 00:28:05.810
Frederick Douglass returned to the United States in the spring of 1860 after Congress

00:28:05.810 --> 00:28:13.840
decided not to pursue Brown's accomplices for fear of creating more martyrs.

00:28:13.840 --> 00:28:20.520
When he arrived, he found the country embroiled in a presidential campaign unlike any other.

00:28:20.520 --> 00:28:26.130
The old political alignments had been destroyed by the argument over slavery, and the election

00:28:26.130 --> 00:28:30.000
was tilting to the candidate of a new party, Republican Abraham.

00:28:30.000 --> 00:28:38.220
Douglass recognizes the Republican party for what it is it's not an abolitionist

00:28:38.220 --> 00:28:45.240
party it's a free soil party committed to the non-extension of slavery he still

00:28:45.240 --> 00:28:51.880
sees that as an enormous triumph for the abolitionist movement Douglas took hope

00:28:51.880 --> 00:29:00.000
in Lincoln's candidacy and publicly endorsed him Garrison was too wary of politics

00:29:00.000 --> 00:29:07.960
to get involved, but he did meet quietly with one of Lincoln's emissaries. William Herndon

00:29:07.960 --> 00:29:13.530
assured him that he and Lincoln were working to the same end. The Republican didn't want

00:29:13.530 --> 00:29:19.300
Garrison's endorsement. In fact, any connection to the controversial printer would be political

00:29:19.300 --> 00:29:25.990
poison. But all the same, Garrison's continued radicalism would be a great help in paving

00:29:25.990 --> 00:29:28.960
the way for a moderate, anti-slavery Republican.

00:29:30.730 --> 00:29:37.680
Garrison maintained his distance, but like every abolitionist, he wondered where all this upheaval would lead.

00:29:37.680 --> 00:29:59.030
During the last few years, free colored people have despaired of their future in the United States.

00:30:02.400 --> 00:30:04.960
They fear that persecution and hardships

00:30:04.960 --> 00:30:10.000
are to grow more and more grievous with every year.

00:30:10.000 --> 00:30:14.670
For this reason, they are now, as never before,

00:30:14.670 --> 00:30:27.110
looking out into the world for a place of retreat and asylum.

00:30:27.110 --> 00:30:30.000
In November of 1860, Abraham Lincoln

00:30:30.000 --> 00:30:36.670
Lincoln was elected president without carrying a single slave-holding state, Washington immediately

00:30:36.670 --> 00:30:43.720
descended into chaos as one southern state after another seceded from the Union.

00:30:43.720 --> 00:30:49.850
Hoping to pacify the South, Congress hurriedly passed a constitutional amendment protecting

00:30:49.850 --> 00:30:55.070
the status of slavery forever.

00:30:55.070 --> 00:31:00.000
Lincoln in his inaugural address approves and supports that amendment.

00:31:00.000 --> 00:31:06.750
vows to add even more teeth to the fugitive slave law. And Douglass reads

00:31:06.750 --> 00:31:13.210
this and says, "I'm out of here." Frederick Douglass's faith in America had

00:31:13.210 --> 00:31:19.370
endured through 20 years of slavery and 20 years of fruitless struggle. But for

00:31:19.370 --> 00:31:22.170
Douglass, Lincoln's effort to placate the slave

00:31:22.170 --> 00:31:30.000
states was one blow too many. On the morning of April 12, 1861, Frederick

00:31:30.000 --> 00:31:34.130
Frederick Douglass was preparing to leave the United States.

00:31:34.130 --> 00:31:38.810
He and his daughter Rosetta had booked passage to Haiti, with an eye to emigrating there

00:31:38.810 --> 00:31:41.210
with his family.

00:31:41.210 --> 00:31:43.890
Their ship was due to sail in 13 days.

00:32:06.780 --> 00:32:07.680
What are you talking about?

00:32:07.680 --> 00:32:13.790
What else did you hear?

00:32:13.790 --> 00:32:25.440
Tell us everything you heard.

00:32:25.440 --> 00:32:28.430
Slowly.

00:32:28.430 --> 00:32:30.000
The creature and his men.

00:32:30.320 --> 00:32:32.880
They can't hold out much longer.

00:32:32.880 --> 00:32:36.570
The president's gonna call out all the troops now.

00:32:36.570 --> 00:32:38.190
It's war for sure.

00:32:38.190 --> 00:32:44.380
That's what everyone's saying.

00:32:44.380 --> 00:32:46.280
- The role that abolitionists played

00:32:46.280 --> 00:32:51.730
in the buildup to the Civil War was absolutely crucial.

00:32:51.730 --> 00:32:54.590
Without the abolitionists,

00:32:54.590 --> 00:32:57.160
the Civil War would not have occurred.

00:32:57.160 --> 00:33:00.000
Without the abolitionists, I think there's

00:33:00.250 --> 00:33:06.130
one could say that slave owners would have turned the entire united states into slave country

00:33:06.130 --> 00:33:14.910
that was what they wanted once the firing happens on fort sumter garrison wraps himself in the flag

00:33:14.910 --> 00:33:23.710
and like practically every other abolitionist endorses the war for the union as a war to

00:33:23.710 --> 00:33:30.000
eliminate slavery garrison like everybody like douglas like all the people that have been

00:33:30.030 --> 00:33:36.630
in tremendous conflict with each other before the war are in a sense reunited in the war

00:33:36.630 --> 00:33:39.670
for the Union.

00:33:39.670 --> 00:33:42.250
Civil War has at last begun.

00:33:42.250 --> 00:33:45.650
A terrible fight is at this moment going on between Fort Sumter and the

00:33:45.650 --> 00:33:49.380
Frederick Douglass canceled his trip to Haiti.

00:33:49.380 --> 00:33:55.770
We shall stay here and watch the current of events and serve the cause of freedom and

00:33:55.770 --> 00:33:59.000
humanity in any way that shall be open to us.

00:34:09.560 --> 00:34:11.540
- With the outbreak of war,

00:34:11.540 --> 00:34:15.000
abolitionists faced a daunting new challenge,

00:34:15.000 --> 00:34:18.060
convincing the president and skeptical northerners

00:34:18.060 --> 00:34:21.440
that the conflict was not about preserving the old union,

00:34:21.440 --> 00:34:26.450
but ending slavery.

00:34:26.450 --> 00:34:30.000
At last, the squabbling abolitionist factions came together

00:34:30.000 --> 00:34:35.350
again, including, above all, Frederick Douglass and William Lloyd Garrison.

00:34:37.550 --> 00:34:43.260
Whatever political or personal differences have divided us, Douglass wrote, a common

00:34:43.260 --> 00:34:47.540
goal makes us forget those differences and strike at the common foe.

00:34:48.740 --> 00:34:54.490
For the first time in years, Douglass and Garrison appeared together in public, and Angelina

00:34:54.490 --> 00:34:58.890
Grimke re-emerged after 20 years' absence to form a women's league.

00:35:02.560 --> 00:35:09.240
But victory, and even an anti-slavery war, turned out to be far more elusive than anyone

00:35:09.240 --> 00:35:12.460
had expected.

00:35:12.460 --> 00:35:18.220
Harriet Beecher Stowe, who had warned of the evils of slavery in Uncle Tom's cabin, saw

00:35:18.220 --> 00:35:22.900
the war as divine retribution.

00:35:22.900 --> 00:35:28.500
It was God's will that the slave mothers, whose tears nobody regarded, should have with

00:35:28.500 --> 00:35:30.000
them a great company of weepers.

00:35:30.000 --> 00:35:36.140
that the free states, who refused to listen when they were told of starvation,

00:35:36.140 --> 00:35:43.610
cold, and barbarous cruelty perpetrated on the slave, should have starvation, cold,

00:35:43.610 --> 00:35:48.520
privation, and barbarous cruelty doing its work among their own sons.

00:35:48.520 --> 00:35:54.970
Stowe spoke from experience. Her son Frederick had rushed to join the

00:35:54.970 --> 00:36:00.000
colors in the first days of the war. Like untold thousands of young men,

00:36:00.670 --> 00:36:04.010
he would never fully recover.

00:36:04.010 --> 00:36:08.370
Time and again, unimaginable slaughter

00:36:08.370 --> 00:36:17.850
was eclipsed within months by something even worse.

00:36:17.850 --> 00:36:26.220
- Abolitionist argument is that all of this blood

00:36:26.220 --> 00:36:29.760
and treasure is gonna be wasted unless slavery dies.

00:36:30.540 --> 00:36:36.590
And more and more and more as the war goes on, it's a realization that many, many Northerners

00:36:36.590 --> 00:36:38.340
come to share.

00:36:38.340 --> 00:36:42.980
Through it all, Lincoln seemed immovable.

00:36:42.980 --> 00:36:50.090
My paramount object in this struggle is to save the Union, he announced in August of 1862,

00:36:50.090 --> 00:37:00.000
and is not either to save or destroy slavery. Many abolitionists lost hope in the president.

00:37:00.000 --> 00:37:02.160
read reports of a meeting at the White House

00:37:02.160 --> 00:37:05.220
later that summer.

00:37:05.220 --> 00:37:07.320
- Lincoln calls in these five ministers

00:37:07.320 --> 00:37:09.950
and he has this back and forth with them,

00:37:09.950 --> 00:37:11.970
mostly gives them a speech.

00:37:11.970 --> 00:37:14.420
He says, without the presence of your people

00:37:14.420 --> 00:37:17.160
in this country, we wouldn't even have this war.

00:37:17.160 --> 00:37:20.960
He tries to recruit these black men to lead efforts

00:37:20.960 --> 00:37:25.360
to colonize black folk out of the United States.

00:37:25.360 --> 00:37:27.600
Lincoln basically says to them,

00:37:27.600 --> 00:37:29.600
this is a single race country.

00:37:30.000 --> 00:37:35.480
The best hope for your people is going to be outside of this country.

00:37:35.480 --> 00:37:41.930
For William Lloyd Garrison and Frederick Douglass, it was the last straw.

00:37:41.930 --> 00:37:43.940
Just God.

00:37:43.940 --> 00:37:49.600
What but the meanest selfishness stimulates this scheme?

00:37:49.600 --> 00:37:55.280
President Lincoln's education with and among the white trash of Kentucky was most unfortunate

00:37:55.280 --> 00:38:00.000
for his moral development, so that there seems to be nothing elevated.

00:38:00.000 --> 00:38:01.640
and noble in his character.

00:38:04.130 --> 00:38:06.410
Mr. Lincoln is a genuine representative

00:38:06.410 --> 00:38:09.730
of American prejudice and Negro hatred

00:38:09.730 --> 00:38:14.190
and far more concerned with the preservation of slavery

00:38:14.190 --> 00:38:17.810
than for any principle of justice and humanity.

00:38:24.840 --> 00:38:28.730
Then, on the 22nd of September, 1862,

00:38:29.560 --> 00:38:30.000
the newspaper...

00:38:30.000 --> 00:38:32.950
The newspapers brought astonishing news.

00:38:32.950 --> 00:38:40.570
Lincoln was promising to sign an Emancipation Proclamation on New Year's Day.

00:38:40.570 --> 00:38:47.390
"All persons," Lincoln wrote, "held as slaves within any state in rebellion shall be then,

00:38:47.390 --> 00:38:52.100
thenceforward, and forever free."

00:38:52.100 --> 00:38:57.760
The proclamation was the first sign that the president was moving by agonizing steps

00:38:57.760 --> 00:38:59.670
to a new understanding of the war.

00:39:02.940 --> 00:39:11.450
But for Lincoln, the carnage had become unendurable, unless it could be given over to a higher purpose.

00:39:11.450 --> 00:39:16.250
Still, Garrison was wary. Lincoln seemed to be hedging.

00:39:16.250 --> 00:39:24.340
In December of 1862, only weeks before the promised date,

00:39:24.340 --> 00:39:30.000
Lincoln presented Congress with a dramatic peace plan: if the southern states would lay down

00:39:30.000 --> 00:39:39.090
their arms, they could keep their slaves for another 40 years. Abolitionists were baffled.

00:39:39.090 --> 00:39:45.540
In the space of five months, the president had declared that the war wasn't about slavery,

00:39:45.540 --> 00:39:50.620
suggested that all black people should leave the United States, promised to emancipate

00:39:50.620 --> 00:39:56.510
the slaves on January 1, and finally offered to let Southerners keep their slaves until

00:39:56.510 --> 00:40:00.000
1900.

00:40:00.380 --> 00:40:05.610
Douglass, and every other abolitionist had good reason to wonder whether Lincoln would

00:40:05.610 --> 00:40:08.230
ever sign the Emancipation Proclamation.

00:40:08.230 --> 00:40:24.390
This day, 1863, was an anxious vigil.

00:40:24.390 --> 00:40:29.570
Tense with anticipation, Boston's abolitionists made their way to two gatherings.

00:40:30.890 --> 00:40:36.440
At the music hall, William Lloyd Garrison and Harriet Beecher Stowe attended a concert

00:40:36.440 --> 00:40:38.860
with some of the city's most distinguished figures.

00:40:42.300 --> 00:40:47.820
Boston's black citizens poured into Tremont Temple to listen to speakers and sing hymns.

00:40:49.390 --> 00:40:54.320
But Frederick Douglass, the man everyone had come to hear, didn't say much of anything

00:40:54.320 --> 00:40:54.800
at all.

00:40:57.640 --> 00:41:00.000
Nine o'clock became ten, and then eleven.

00:41:00.000 --> 00:41:06.070
and still there was no word Douglas remembered being unable to listen to the

00:41:06.070 --> 00:41:13.470
speakers as he kept his eye on the door finally near midnight a messenger rushed

00:41:13.470 --> 00:41:25.250
onto the stage Lincoln had kept his word every enslaved person in the south was

00:41:25.250 --> 00:41:28.870
then thenceforward and forever free

00:41:33.930 --> 00:41:37.690
That was, in some ways, the turning point of Frederick Douglass' life,

00:41:38.540 --> 00:41:43.270
and certainly the turning point in the historical life of African Americans.

00:41:44.940 --> 00:41:48.420
A new country, a new constitution, a new history is going to come out of this.

00:41:51.850 --> 00:41:53.650
I never saw enthusiasm before.

00:41:55.060 --> 00:41:56.800
I never saw joy before.

00:41:58.840 --> 00:42:00.000
Henceforth, this day,

00:42:00.730 --> 00:42:07.980
shall take rank with the 4th of July, henceforth it becomes the date of a new and glorious

00:42:07.980 --> 00:42:23.300
era in the history of American liberty. With the signing of the Emancipation Proclamation,

00:42:23.300 --> 00:42:30.000
the war merged with the abolitionist cause, but the union had to win, or the proclamation

00:42:30.000 --> 00:42:39.220
would mean nothing. The proclamation had included an unexpected clause. At last, black men could

00:42:39.220 --> 00:42:46.930
enlist in the armed services of the United States. As Douglass said, Lincoln's paper proclamation

00:42:46.930 --> 00:43:00.000
must now be made iron, lead, and fire. Douglass enlisted two of his sons, and even William Lloyd

00:43:00.000 --> 00:43:07.740
David Garrison, the ultra-peaceman, allowed his firstborn to join the colors.

00:43:07.740 --> 00:43:17.410
The Civil War moved Garrison farther and farther away from that abstract devotion to

00:43:17.410 --> 00:43:22.530
peace principles and pacifism that he had held through the years.

00:43:22.530 --> 00:43:28.260
When Garrison is forced to choose, he chooses abolition over his peace principles.

00:43:31.720 --> 00:43:39.500
For Douglass, what better symbol of democracy and of racial equality is there in the United

00:43:39.500 --> 00:43:47.420
States is if the federal government arms and gives uniforms to black soldiers and tells

00:43:47.420 --> 00:43:51.080
them to kill white men.

00:43:51.080 --> 00:43:54.400
The one-time radicals were no longer outsiders.

00:43:54.400 --> 00:43:59.350
Garrison, Douglass, and Stowe would each meet the president at the White House.

00:44:00.000 --> 00:44:06.580
gave him their heartfelt support. Suddenly abolitionists are looking

00:44:06.580 --> 00:44:14.010
prophetic. They seem now to be having had anticipated all of these things that are

00:44:14.010 --> 00:44:20.200
happening and abolitionists are accorded a newfound respect that they had never

00:44:20.200 --> 00:44:26.960
experienced before. But victory was nowhere in sight. The

00:44:26.960 --> 00:44:30.000
slaughter of the first two years of war was but a

00:44:30.000 --> 00:44:37.140
prelude. Hundreds of thousands of men, white and black, would be swallowed up in the abyss.

00:44:38.260 --> 00:44:43.900
Yet for Douglas, Garrison, and Stowe, for millions of slaves and millions of their white

00:44:43.900 --> 00:44:50.740
brethren, all of the suffering finally had a new meaning. Victory meant abolition.

00:44:54.070 --> 00:45:00.000
If we fail, we shall go down as we deserve to go down. As a warning to all other nations,

00:45:00.000 --> 00:45:08.990
nations which shall come after us, but if we succeed, our glory as a nation will be complete.

00:45:08.990 --> 00:45:15.460
Our peace will flow like a river, and our foundations will be the everlasting rocks.

00:45:32.300 --> 00:45:37.310
President Lincoln himself had suggested that Garrison come to Charleston.

00:45:37.310 --> 00:45:45.190
The editor was overwhelmed as he walked among the ruins of the once gracious city.

00:45:45.190 --> 00:45:51.960
The news of Robert E. Lee's surrender at Appomattox came the day of Garrison's arrival.

00:45:51.960 --> 00:45:56.160
The war was over.

00:45:56.160 --> 00:46:00.000
It had been four years to the day since federal troops evacuated something.

00:46:01.400 --> 00:46:07.400
Lincoln thought Garrison should have the honor of helping raise the flag over the reconquered fortress.

00:46:07.400 --> 00:46:15.740
At Fort Sumter, Garrison reminisced about the 30-year anti-slavery struggle,

00:46:15.740 --> 00:46:18.740
marveling at the transformation that had taken place.

00:46:18.740 --> 00:46:22.390
His reservations about Lincoln had vanished entirely.

00:46:22.390 --> 00:46:30.000
"Either he's become a Garrisonian abolitionist," Garrison said, "or I have become a Lincoln abolitionist."

00:46:30.000 --> 00:46:40.710
emancipationist, for we blend together like kindred drops into one. For his part, a few

00:46:40.710 --> 00:46:46.530
days earlier, Lincoln had told an admirer that he had been only an instrument in the

00:46:46.530 --> 00:46:54.780
anti-slavery struggle. As he put it, the logic and moral power of Garrison and the anti-slavery

00:46:54.780 --> 00:46:56.630
people had done it all.

00:47:01.440 --> 00:47:10.040
But for Garrison, the real triumph came the next morning, at a mass meeting of freed slaves.

00:47:10.040 --> 00:47:17.080
A crowd estimated at 10,000 had gathered outside the Charleston Citadel.

00:47:17.080 --> 00:47:22.260
When Garrison appeared, the freedmen seized him joyfully and carried him on their shoulders

00:47:22.260 --> 00:47:24.620
around the square.

00:47:24.620 --> 00:47:30.000
They then proceeded a few blocks to Zion's church, where a mass of humanity pressed in

00:47:30.000 --> 00:47:37.990
into every available spot. Once Garrison and the other dignitaries had been seated at the

00:47:37.990 --> 00:47:45.680
pulpit, a black man and his two daughters approached. Sir, he addressed Garrison as the

00:47:45.680 --> 00:47:53.390
church fell silent, here you see your handiwork. These children were robbed from me, and I

00:47:53.390 --> 00:48:00.000
stood desolate. You have restored them to me, as you have restored other mothers to

00:48:00.280 --> 00:48:09.600
Fathers, sisters, brothers, the greeting that they would give you is almost impossible for me to express.

00:48:09.600 --> 00:48:15.430
Simply, sir, we welcome and look upon you as our savior.

00:48:15.430 --> 00:48:29.000
I have been actively engaged in this work for almost 40 years, but I never expected to look you in the face.

00:48:30.000 --> 00:48:36.540
supposed you would hear of anything I might do in your behalf. I have no

00:48:36.540 --> 00:48:51.280
language to express the feelings of my heart." Lincoln's Emancipation

00:48:51.280 --> 00:48:55.950
Proclamation had freed the slaves, but it hadn't outlawed slavery itself

00:48:55.950 --> 00:49:00.000
anywhere. That could be accomplished only by

00:49:00.000 --> 00:49:08.150
amending the Constitution. In December of 1865, the United States was finally

00:49:08.150 --> 00:49:12.970
cleansed of its original sin when the 13th Amendment was added to the

00:49:12.970 --> 00:49:25.150
Constitution, banning slavery in all the states forever. For almost four

00:49:25.150 --> 00:49:29.060
decades, Garrison had dedicated his life to this moment.

00:49:30.000 --> 00:49:35.400
He had created a movement, had led it through every adversity without wavering.

00:49:35.400 --> 00:49:41.010
The Liberator had become not only the most influential voice of abolition, but the symbol

00:49:41.010 --> 00:49:51.680
of its tenacity: 1,803 issues, every one set by hand, most by Garrison's own.

00:49:51.680 --> 00:49:57.780
And this, Garrison felt, was a fitting occasion to print the last.

00:49:57.780 --> 00:49:59.700
The post-war years would be hard.

00:50:00.640 --> 00:50:05.280
Garrison, Douglas, and their kindred spirits protested in vain

00:50:05.280 --> 00:50:10.370
as northern and southern whites conspired to keep the emancipated slaves

00:50:10.370 --> 00:50:14.220
in a condition almost indistinguishable from slavery.

00:50:14.220 --> 00:50:20.200
But nothing could entirely erase the change that had taken place in American life.

00:50:20.200 --> 00:50:29.860
When Garrison died in 1879 at the age of 73,

00:50:30.640 --> 00:50:36.770
Frederick Douglass was transported to the days of his youth, to the days when, as a newly

00:50:36.770 --> 00:50:42.780
escaped slave, he had first heard William Lloyd Garrison and thought him the new Moses.

00:50:47.330 --> 00:50:50.250
His eulogy was more than a memorial to one man.

00:50:51.560 --> 00:50:53.640
Angelina Grimke was on her deathbed.

00:50:55.380 --> 00:50:57.020
John Brown was in his heaven.

00:50:59.180 --> 00:51:00.000
Harriet Beecher's son.

00:51:00.000 --> 00:51:02.000
had withdrawn from public life.

00:51:02.000 --> 00:51:07.480
Douglas would write a lament for the movement they had led,

00:51:07.480 --> 00:51:12.370
for their youth, for the generation that was passing from the scene.

00:51:12.370 --> 00:51:18.330
It was a tribute to fervent ideals, generosity, and love,

00:51:18.330 --> 00:51:23.120
to the bitterness and the passions that had moved men and women

00:51:23.120 --> 00:51:28.230
to bend the arc of history.

00:51:28.230 --> 00:51:30.000
The abolitionists of this country

00:51:30.250 --> 00:51:37.710
never a numerous class but lately death has been busy in reducing their already thin ranks

00:51:37.710 --> 00:51:44.360
only a few can now tell from actual experience something of the darkness and peril that brooded

00:51:44.360 --> 00:51:51.900
over the land when the anti-slavery movement was born now the brightest and steadiest of all the

00:51:51.900 --> 00:51:59.760
shining hosts of our moral sky has silently and peacefully descended below the distant horizon

00:52:02.560 --> 00:52:07.270
He moved not with the tide, but against it.

00:52:07.270 --> 00:52:11.080
He rose not by the power of the church or the state,

00:52:11.080 --> 00:52:14.680
but in bold, inflexible and defiant opposition

00:52:14.680 --> 00:52:17.670
to the mighty power of both.

00:52:17.670 --> 00:52:19.910
It was the glory of this man

00:52:19.910 --> 00:52:22.630
that he could stand alone with the truth

00:52:22.630 --> 00:52:25.990
and calmly await the result.

00:52:25.990 --> 00:52:29.600
Now that this man has filled up the measure of his years,

00:52:30.640 --> 00:52:34.560
Now that the leaf has fallen to the ground as all leaves must fall,

00:52:35.100 --> 00:52:36.380
let us guard his memory.

00:52:37.720 --> 00:52:41.800
Let us try to imitate his virtues and endeavor as he did

00:52:41.800 --> 00:52:49.360
to leave the world freer, nobler, and better than we found it.

